    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Confucianism grew from the teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, a scholar of the state of Lu who taught that a good society rests on humane, cultivated people who honour their families, observe ritual and govern by virtue.</li><li>Adopted by the Han emperors and later made the basis of the civil service examinations, it was the official orthodoxy of the Chinese empire for some two thousand years and the model of government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</li><li>Attacked as a cause of backwardness in the 20th century, it has revived since the 1980s, and its ideals of learning, family duty and moral government remain deeply rooted in East Asian societies.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> have kept their family records for more than eighty generations: their genealogy is often described as the longest recorded family tree in the world.</p></aside>
    </section>

